Printing spread slowly northward from the workshops of the Rhine valley, carried by merchants who had seen a commercial opportunity in the new craft. A single press could produce in a week what a scribe needed a season to copy, and the price of a book fell fast enough that town councils began to worry about the flood of unlicensed texts. Guilds formed, apprentices were bound for seven years, and disputes over type, paper and the right to sell in a distant market filled the municipal records. By the middle of the sixteenth century every city of any size had at least one printer, and many had three or four competing for the same small readership.

The books themselves changed as the trade matured. Early editions imitated the manuscripts they replaced, down to the scribal abbreviations and the ruled margins, because buyers trusted what they recognised. Later printers, less attached to the old conventions, began to number the pages, to divide chapters more evenly and to set indexes at the back. These small decisions, repeated across thousands of volumes, fixed habits of reading that survived long after the hand press disappeared. A reader two centuries later expected a table of contents, running heads and a title page carrying the name of the publisher; few of these were obvious inventions at the outset.

Censorship followed the trade as well. Authorities that had never troubled themselves over a single manuscript now faced hundreds of identical copies moving through the same channels, and they answered with lists of prohibited titles, privileges granted to favoured shops and inspections at the city gates. The effect was rarely what the censors intended. Printers moved to neighbouring towns, editions appeared with false imprints, and texts that might have vanished in a single copy survived in dozens of surreptitious ones. Historians who work on this period have learned to distrust the title page and to read the paper, the watermark and the binding before they trust the date.

The trade also produced its own literature. Manuals explained how to cast type, how to damp paper evenly and how to keep a press true through the winter. Correspondence between firms records orders for alphabets in Greek and Hebrew, complaints about the cost of good ink and the perennial problem of finding a compositor who could read more than one language. Few of these documents were meant for posterity, which is part of why they are so useful: they describe the workshop as it was, not as the printer wished it to appear.

Paper was the largest recurring expense and the least controllable. Rags were collected door to door, sorted by quality, fermented and beaten into pulp, then dried on frames that left the faint lines still visible in surviving sheets. A shortage of rags could idle a shop for weeks, and the search for substitutes occupied inventors well into the following century. Watermarks, pressed into the wet pulp, were the closest thing the trade had to a signature, and a careful scholar can often date a sheet within a few decades from the mark alone.

